# Target C: quantitative conditioning of the strong open-cell staircase

Date: 2026-10-04; finite-isolation corollary added 2026-10-05
Base main: 55ae751c8d599fb52611c0d77f9d3a17ac2990ad
Additional source: `automation/target-c-open-cell-strong-scc-multishear-rigidity-2026-10-04-v2` at `bd70618a69dae3664cefeb1275bfedde771eed78`
Status: proof-only Target C-II candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.
LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

This note continues only the nonconstant open-witness-cell strong-SCC route on current main. It replaces the qualitative injectivity of the staircase diameter constraints by an explicit source-independent conditioning bound.

No computation, verifier, or CI result is a proof premise.

## 1. Setup

Let the selected cuts be \(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m\), with unit train directions \(u_i\). Current main gives one common maximal staircase of nonempty actual diameter supports
\[
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_m,\qquad |I_j|=m-j+1.
\tag{1}
\]

Every support type \(I_j\) has at least one physical actual-diameter row of each strict transverse sign. Choose one representative of each sign and denote their oriented diameter vectors by
\[
r_j^+,\qquad r_j^-,\qquad |r_j^\pm|=D.
\tag{2}
\]

They are distinct actual diameter edges. Canonical C67 gives projective direction separation
\[
\alpha_j:=
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(\operatorname{dir}r_j^+,\operatorname{dir}r_j^-)
\ge \delta_D,\qquad
\delta_D=2\arcsin {1\over 2D}.
\tag{3}
\]

For an infinitesimal exact laminar multishear \(c=(c_1,\ldots,c_m)\), put
\[
Z_j(c):=\sum_{i\in I_j}c_i u_i.
\tag{4}
\]

The squared-length first variation of a row \(h\) with support \(I_j\) is
\[
\ell_h(c)=2r_h\cdot JZ_j(c).
\tag{5}
\]

## 2. A C67-conditioned two-row estimate

Let \(p,q\) be two vectors of length \(D\), with projective line-angle \(\alpha\in(0,\pi/2]\). Then for every \(Z\in\mathbb R^2\),
\[
\max\{|p\cdot JZ|,\ |q\cdot JZ|\}
\ge D\sin(\alpha/2)\,|Z|.
\tag{6}
\]

After changing the sign of \(q\) if necessary, the ordinary angle between \(p/D\) and \(q/D\) is \(\alpha\). The smallest singular value of the two-row matrix with rows \(p/D\) and \(q/D\) is
\[
\sqrt{1-\cos\alpha}=\sqrt2\sin(\alpha/2).
\]
Passing from the Euclidean norm of the two outputs to their maximum divides by at most \(\sqrt2\), proving (6). The rotation \(J\) does not change \(|Z|\).

Apply (6) to the pair (2). By (3),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\max\{|\ell_{r_j^+}(c)|,|\ell_{r_j^-}(c)|\}
&\ge 2D\sin(\delta_D/2)\,|Z_j(c)|\\
&=|Z_j(c)|,
\end{aligned}
\tag{7}
\]
because
\[
2D\sin(\delta_D/2)
=
2D\sin\left(\arcsin{1\over2D}\right)
=1.
\tag{8}
\]

Thus every staircase support type comes with a two-row measurement whose conditioning constant is at least one:
\[
\boxed{
\max\{|\ell_{r_j^+}(c)|,|\ell_{r_j^-}(c)|\}
\ge |Z_j(c)|.
}
\tag{9}
\]

This bound is uniform in \(D\), in the width of the open phase cell, and in the KKT coefficients.

## 3. The maximal staircase inverts with loss at most two

Let \(\delta_j\) be the cut deleted from \(I_j\) to \(I_{j+1}\), and let \(I_m=\{\delta_m\}\). Put \(Z_{m+1}=0\). Then
\[
c_{\delta_j}u_{\delta_j}=Z_j-Z_{j+1}
\qquad(1\le j\le m).
\tag{10}
\]

Hence, if
\[
M(c):=\max_{1\le j\le m}|Z_j(c)|,
\tag{11}
\]
then
\[
|c_{\delta_j}|
\le |Z_j|+|Z_{j+1}|
\le 2M(c)
\tag{12}
\]
for \(j<m\), while \(|c_{\delta_m}|=|Z_m|\le M(c)\). Since \(\delta_1,\ldots,\delta_m\) is a permutation of the selected cuts,
\[
\boxed{
M(c)\ge {1\over2}\|c\|_\infty.
}
\tag{13}
\]

Combining (9) and (13), among the \(2m\) chosen staircase representatives,
\[
\boxed{
\max_h |\ell_h(c)|
\ge {1\over2}\|c\|_\infty
\ge {1\over2\sqrt m}\|c\|_2.
}
\tag{14}
\]

This is an explicit inverse bound for the full strong-SCC staircase measurement map. In particular, it is injective without a source-dependent compactness argument.

## 4. Consequence for one-sided diameter descent

Current main has the positive KKT cut-resultant identities
\[
A_i u_i=\sum_{h:\,i\in I_h}\beta_h r_h,
\qquad A_i>0,\quad \beta_h>0.
\tag{15}
\]

Multiplying by the laminar shear coefficients, rotating by \(J\), summing over cuts, and regrouping by diameter rows gives
\[
\sum_h\beta_h\ell_h(c)=0.
\tag{16}
\]

Suppose every old actual diameter is first-order nonlengthening: \(\ell_h(c)\le0\) for all \(h\). Positivity of every \(\beta_h\) in (16) forces \(\ell_h(c)=0\) for all actual diameters. Equation (14) then forces \(c=0\).

Therefore
\[
\boxed{
c\ne0
\Longrightarrow
\text{some old actual diameter lengthens to first order and some old actual diameter shortens to first order.}
}
\tag{17}
\]

The new point over the qualitative tangent-cone statement is (14): the absolute first-order diameter response of the selected staircase rows cannot collapse faster than \(\|c\|_\infty/2\).

There is deliberately no claimed uniform lower bound on the positive response alone. Equation (16) contains KKT weights with no proved source-independent positive lower bound, so the quantitatively large response supplied by (14) could a priori be on the shortening side while the compensating lengthening response has very small unweighted magnitude.

### Source-dependent finite local isolation

For each fixed source and normalized stress, define

\[
\gamma_+ := \min_{\|c\|_2=1}\max_h\ell_h(c),\qquad
\gamma_- := \min_{\|c\|_2=1}\max_h(-\ell_h(c)).
\]

The one-sided conclusion above makes both functions strictly positive on the
unit sphere: if every \(\ell_h(c)\le0\), positivity of all \(\beta_h\) and
(16) force every \(\ell_h(c)=0\), and the injectivity in (14) forces \(c=0\).
The reversed-sign argument is identical. Since the actual diameter set is
finite, continuity and compactness give \(\gamma_+,\gamma_->0\). They are
source-dependent; no source-independent lower bound is asserted.

For the exact finite laminar shear parameter \(\theta\), each actual diameter
has the Taylor expansion
\[
|r_h(\theta)|^2-D^2=\ell_h(\theta)+O(\|\theta\|_2^2),
\]
uniformly over the finite row set. Consequently every sufficiently small
nonzero \(\theta\) has an old actual diameter that lengthens and an old one that
shortens. In particular the origin is isolated among diameter-feasible points
of this exact shear family. This is a local source-level conclusion only; it
does not construct a nontrivial feasible endpoint or exclude contact-changing
moves outside the family.

## 5. Target-C effect

The strong open-cell branch is quantitatively rigid inside the exact laminar shear coordinates:

- the \(2m\) paired staircase rows define an injective first-variation map;
- its inverse \(\ell_\infty\)-conditioning loss is at most two;
- C67 supplies the uniform constant, so no compactness or unproved multiplier floor is needed.

This does not close C-II. It removes a degeneracy in any finite contact-changing argument built around the staircase: a proposed shear cannot hide in an arbitrarily ill-conditioned first-order kernel. The remaining issue is one-sided. A closing argument must either obtain a lower bound on the lengthening side from source/contact currency, or leave the laminar shear family and construct a nonlocal contact-changing competitor. The separate condensation alternative remains the inverse-\(D\) inactive near-unit or near-diameter witness.

Disposition: proof-only Target C candidate. C-II and Level I remain open.
